{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested. No behavioural defect was found: Architecture and Security both pass, replay parity is proven rather than argued, and every gate is green at 768582b1. The requested changes are confined to two Simplification removals.\n\nARCHITECTURE - pass. Layers touched: 3 (internal/kernel), 4 (internal/workroom plus the host-binding vocabulary) and 5 (internal/app), with the 6/7 surfaces in internal/service and cmd/gs. The layer 4/5 contract change is real and is recorded in the same head. fold-activation leaves the starter catalog (13 definitions to 12); a state@2 record naming it decides undefined-kind and appends no FoldTransition; the bridge admits it only for state@0/state@1, where it reproduces the old constraint set exactly. \"Fold upgrades are host-binding replacements\" describes code that already exists rather than an aspiration: readBinding keeps the last binding record signed by the initializing actor key, so bootstrap and replacement are one rule, and a build only selects an interpreter it holds. The kernel stays schema-agnostic - checkpoint@3 removes a coupling instead of adding fold awareness. docs/reference/architecture.md records the change at every place it shows: the kernel bullet, a new checkpoint@3 paragraph with the v1/v2 read policy, the fold-upgrade-is-a-binding-replacement paragraph, the state@2/ratify@1 and migration-bridge paragraphs, and both the internal/kernel and internal/app table rows. merge.md, limits.md, deploy-a-resident.md and SKILL.md agree with it. fold@4 to fold@5 is now only the application projection cache key, gated as application + NUL + foldVersion: strictly narrower than the old global checkpoint key, and it additionally separates two applications at one fold version. cmd/gs/succession.go changes only a comment; planSuccession reads projection.Artifacts, which is schema-independent, and all 1113 live artifacts project identically under both binaries, so state@0 and state@1 artifact acts remain recognized.\n\nSECURITY, FOLD-ACTIVATION AUTHORITY - pass. (a) A state@2 record can no longer obtain a fold-activation definition at all: the kind is out of the catalog and the bridge is gated on state@0/state@1, so it decides undefined-kind and no transition is appended. (b) No residual path smuggles a fold through a kind definition: KindDefinition carries no Fold field (the remaining Fold in kinds.go is FoldTransition, the historical activation record), decodeKindDefinition refuses body.fold outright, and the name stays reserved in foldInterpreted so no declaration may rebind it. (c) ratify@1 of any activation is Ineffective, \"fold activation moved to the host binding\"; the added type assertion is safe because the *State check guards it three lines above. A new state@1 activation can still be fold-effective, but nothing can bring it into force: only ratify@0 could ratify it, and admission refuses exactly that. (d) Application admission refuses both halves independently - a state@0/state@1 submission naming fold-activation, and any ratification whose target is a fold-activation statement - and the refused submission provably does not advance verified history.\n\nSECURITY, CHECKPOINT BOUNDARY - pass. Distinct conclusion, measured against checkpoint v2. (a) What left the checkpoint is the application Profile. Cross-profile substitution is now possible by design and is sound, because the checkpoint carries only kernel-verified event material, so any fold re-derives the same projection from it; TestProjectionProfileChangeRebuildsFromTheSameKernelCheckpoint shows the projection identical after a profile change and sourced from the signed checkpoint tail. Cross-repository substitution is still refused: genesis, object format and the descriptor must agree, and the stored events must bind into the real named first-parent sequence, so a checkpoint from another log or a fork cannot be loaded. (b) Signed coverage is complete after the field change. The marker now selects the schema branch, and the marker lives in the commit message inside the signed commit object, so it cannot be flipped; marker and schema must agree or the default arm refuses. Signature verification still precedes payload decompression, and the sequencer key is still derived through authenticated rotations from the genesis descriptor. (c) v1 and v2 on disk are accepted after full authentication with their historical profile ignored, and each is refused when that field is missing; a v3 carrying a profile is refused. So the upgrade itself needs no cold audit. The window runs the other way: a binary older than this head cannot read a v3 marker and cold-audits once - recoverable and non-corrupting, cheap at this depth, and merge.md already directs restarting every resident and adapter at the merged commit. (d) TestWrittenCheckpointContainsOnlyKernelVerifiedMaterial is the guard the design note demanded, and it is closed-world: exactly six manifest fields, none of profile, projection, vocabulary or fold. A future optimization that cached fold output inside the checkpoint fails that test rather than silently making cross-fold reuse unsound.\n\nREPLAY PARITY - proven. I built gs from main 84805b04 and from 768582b1 and ran both against this live log. All 6061 decisions are byte-identical, as are actors, acts, artifacts, commitments, provenance, reviews, statements, omitted_supersessions and opaque_kinds. The only difference anywhere is vocabulary.definitions losing fold-activation, which is the intended change. The equivalence is also exact by construction: the artifact-path rule moved from schema == state@1 to schema != state@0, the same predicate for every record that exists. This log holds zero fold-activation, zero kind-def and zero body.fold records, so neither the bridge nor the new body.fold refusal can rewrite any historical decision here.\n\nMUTATIONS - ten guards broken one at a time, nine caught. Caught: skipping checkpoint signature verification; accepting a profile inside a v3 checkpoint; writing the profile back into the checkpoint; ratify@1 ratifying an activation; a kind definition carrying a fold pointer; extending the bridge to state@2; admitting a legacy activation; admitting a new ratification of one; dropping the profile gate from the projection cache key. One survived: removing stored.Genesis != genesis from readCheckpointCandidate leaves the kernel package green. That is not a regression from this head - the identical mutation on main also survives - and it is shadowed by the deeper binding, since stored events must still bind into the real named sequence. It deserves a test under its own request, not a change here.\n\nGATES at the exact head - go build ./... and go vet ./... clean; go test ./... -count=1 green across 25 packages with no failures, so main's earlier README red is cured at this head; go test -race clean on internal/workroom, internal/kernel, internal/service and internal/app, covering the concurrent publication path. Head equals origin/request/schema-fold-split, the worktree is clean, the diff against merge-base 84805b04 is exactly the authorized 20 files, and the head is not an ancestor of main - no merge occurred.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION - two changes requested. Both are removals, both mechanical, neither weakens a condition of satisfaction or a test.\n\n1. CheckpointOptions.Profile and Options.CheckpointProfile are retained \"for source compatibility with older embedders\". No such embedder can exist: internal/kernel is an internal package of github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq and is unimportable outside the module. The only production caller sets Enabled: true and never Profile; every remaining use is a test. The retention costs a doubled enable path, enabled() = Enabled || Profile != \"\", and a field whose name still promises the isolation it no longer provides, inside the kernel's most security-sensitive struct. The rationale comment is the part that most needs to go: a future reader will preserve the field because of it, and a future caller setting Profile will get an enabled, unkeyed cache. Please drop both fields, keep Enabled alone, and update the roughly forty test call sites to CheckpointOptions{Enabled: true} - a rename that removes no coverage. This is the split's own principle: delete the authority path rather than leave an alias of the one removed.\n\n2. SetRebuildTestGate and its rebuildTestGate field now have no caller anywhere in the repository. The service test that drove them was rewritten onto SetProjectionRebuildTestGate, and that rewrite also dropped the only exercise of /v0/rebuild in its Running: true state - moving Verified, Total stable across polls, and no restart after the first reader cancels. Cold full audit is still covered at the app layer through SnapshotSourceColdFullAudit, and the bounded field set is structurally guaranteed by the three-field rebuildReport literal, so what is actually lost is the progress reporting during a cold audit, on a path that still runs in production whenever no usable checkpoint exists. Please either restore that coverage against a genuinely cold audit or delete the dead gate. The head currently keeps the worst of both: an unused exported hook beside an untested live path.\n\nEverything else reads as clean removal rather than a parallel path: the historical bridge is one ten-line function, the export surface grows by a single test gate, and no doubled fold-activation implementation is left behind. Fix these two and I expect to approve the next head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:17e5664c76e6d0e44e559cccc273ff9c2f7ac0b5","head":"768582b124ad2b54ede3609ca58ab7b68f921907","verdict":"changes-requested"}}